<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Redemption Song</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written c. 1979; released <a href="/years/1980">1980</a> on the album Uprising</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Redemption <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; is the last track of Uprising, the twelfth album of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley">Bob Marley</a> and the Wailers, produced by Chris Blackwell and released by Island Records in 1980. It is reported to have been written around 1979 and first appears on a demo tape known as the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dada">Dada</a> Demos&quot;.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Some of the song's key lines come from a speech by the Pan-Africanist orator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a>, given at Menelik Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney, Nova Scotia</a>, in October 1937 and published in his Black Man magazine. Garvey told his audience that they would emancipate themselves from mental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, because while others might free the body, none but themselves could free the mind. Marley recited the speech in public as early as July 1979, during his appearance at the Amandla Festival.</p><p>By then Marley had been diagnosed with the cancer in his toe that killed him in 1981. According to Rita Marley, he was already secretly in a lot of pain and dealing with his own mortality, which she found clearly apparent in the album and above all in this song.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Unlike most of Marley's recordings, the album version is a solo acoustic performance in G major, his voice and an acoustic guitar with no accompaniment and no reggae rhythm. Its best-known lines urge listeners to &quot;Emancipate yourself from mental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>&quot;, because &quot;None but ourselves can free our minds&quot;. Biographer Timothy White called it an acoustic spiritual, and Stephen Davis a total departure.</p><p>Marley first played it in public at the opening show of the Uprising Tour in Zürich on 30 May 1980, and it was in every known setlist of that tour, performed with the full band. A full band version was also issued on the single released in the UK and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in October 1980, later added to the 2001 reissue of Uprising, but the solo take remains the one most listeners know.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 2009 the Jamaican poet and broadcaster Mutabaruka chose it as the most influential recording in Jamaican music history. It has been recorded by Manfred Mann's Earth Band in 1983 and by Stevie Wonder in 1996, and Joe Strummer's version with the Mescaleros appeared on Streetcore in 2003, after his death; Strummer also sang it as a duet with Johnny Cash. On 5 February 2020, the eve of what would have been his 75th birthday, Marley's estate released an animated video for the song.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Redemption Song” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
